  "verbalization": "Etodolac, a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory compound, is consistently linked to Cyp2c9 through its inclusion in the Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Compounds class, which includes oxaprozin, meloxicam, and mefenamic acid. These compounds all bind to Cyp2c9, suggesting a shared mechanism of action involving cytochrome P450 2C9. The lowest common ancestors of Etodolac and these drugs—Nonsteroidal Antiinflammatory Drug and Organic Heteropolycyclic Compound—highlight a broader category of compounds that share structural or functional properties. The evidence suggests that Etodolac may act through a pathway that involves the same metabolic enzyme, Cyp2c9, as other NSAIDs, potentially through a common mechanism of enzyme inhibition or regulation. The integration of these paths underscores the role of Cyp2c9 in the metabolism of NSAIDs, with Etodolac possibly contributing to this process through its inclusion in the same class of compounds. The relationship is supported by the recurring binding of these drugs to Cyp2c9, reinforcing the likelihood of a direct or indirect interaction between Etodolac and the enzyme.",
